3. Explain in your own words the difference between DNS and DHCP, and give a real-world example of how each is used in an app like Zomato or Flipkart.
==>DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) is the service that automatically hands out IP addresses, subnet masks, gateways and DNS server addresses to devices when they join a network.
DNS (Domain Name System) is the phone-book that translates human-readable names (zomato.com) into the numeric IP addresses computers actually use.
Real-world examples:

Zomato / Flipkart – DHCP: When your phone joins home Wi-Fi or mobile data, the router/carrier’s DHCP server gives it a temporary IP so it can talk on the network. You never type an IP yourself.
Zomato / Flipkart – DNS: When the app (or browser) requests https://www.zomato.com or https://www.flipkart.com, DNS resolves those names to the real server IPs so the connection can be made. Without DNS you would have to remember long numbers instead of domain names.

